<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Discovering Our Classroom – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Book Is on the Table.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Explain keywords from readings in simpler terms. Communication: Facilitate basic peer interactions in group activities.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain keywords about classroom items with gestures (e.g., points to a "pencil" and says "write with it"). Can help peers ask questions about classroom items by pointing or showing objects (e.g., "What's in your bag?" by pointing to a bag). Can clarify meanings by pointing to or holding objects (e.g., holds a "book" and says, "This is for reading.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Find the Object" game. Students listen as the teacher calls out a classroom item (e.g., "Find a book!") and points to it or touches it. Then, the teacher says a preposition (e.g., "on the table"). Students quickly point to a book on a table. This activates vocabulary and prepositions learned in previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a "pencil" and says, "This is a pencil. We write with it." (modeling mediation of meaning). The teacher points to a "board" and says, "The board is behind me." The teacher exaggerates /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "table", saying "B-book" and "T-table" for emphasis. The teacher also models how to explain keywords: "pencil – you write with it" using gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds a "ruler" and asks: "Is this for writing?" (Expected answer: No.) "What is this for?" (Expected answer: Drawing lines/measuring.) The teacher then asks: "Is the book on the chair?" (Expected answer: No, it's on the desk.) This checks understanding of verbs and prepositions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large colorful classroom poster or a short illustrated story about two friends finding items in their classroom. The poster/story shows various classroom items with their locations (e.g., "The book is on the shelf," "The crayons are in the pencil case," "The map is behind the board.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Locating objects and places: Using appropriate prepositions to describe familiar object places (e.g., "The book is on the table.") Confirming object locations in the classroom: "It's here." "It's on the shelf."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to items on the poster/in the story and asks, "What's this?" and "Where is the [item]?" Students respond with simple sentences like, "It's a book. It's on the shelf."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks yes/no questions: "Is the ruler in the desk?" (Yes/No).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match picture cards of items to their correct locations on a smaller version of the poster.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> WH-questions ("What's in your bag?"), Prepositions of place ("on the table"), Present simple ("The book is..."), classroom vocabulary (desk, book, pencil, shelf, etc.), verbs (write, read, draw).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Keyword Clarification Match (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive worksheets with common classroom items (e.g., "pencil," "book," "eraser," "crayon") on one side and simple explanations/actions on the other (e.g., "write with it," "read it," "erase mistakes," "color pictures"). Students draw lines to match each item with its explanation. Teacher monitors and checks for understanding of blending in "on" and "paper."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Explain It!" Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> In small groups, students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by holding an object (e.g., a "pen") and says, "This is a pen. We write with it." The teacher passes the pen to the next student, who holds a different object (e.g., a "notebook") and says, "This is a notebook. We write in it." The game continues with students explaining what each object is used for, using simple sentences and gestures, mediating the meaning to their peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Location Mediation (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student has 3-4 classroom items (e.g., a book, a ruler, a pencil case). Student A places their items in various locations on their desk (e.g., "The book is next to the ruler."). Student B asks, "Where is the book?" Student A explains the location. Then, Student A picks an item and asks, "What is this for?" Student B clarifies its use. Roles then switch. Teacher encourages use of "Excuse me..." for respectful questions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group "Object Sorting Game" (Project 2). Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of picture cards depicting various classroom items (desk, book, marker, board, shelf, eraser, paper, chair, crayon, notebook, ruler, pencil, pencil case, pen, map). They also receive larger cards with preposition categories (e.g., "ON", "IN", "NEXT TO", "BEHIND").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students categorize the item picture cards under the correct preposition labels, deciding where each item would logically be found or placed (e.g., "book" under "ON" or "IN").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once sorted, each group member takes a turn to "mediate" and present their sorted items to another group or the whole class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For example, one student points to the "ON" category and says, "The book is on the table. The ruler is on the desk." Another student from the same group holds up a "pencil" card and says, "This is a pencil. We write with it." (explaining the keyword).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Peers can ask, "What is 'marker'?" and the student must explain, "It's for writing on the board." or "It's a big pen for the board." using gestures and simple words to clarify. The emphasis is on facilitating understanding among group members and between groups.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "Object Sorting Game" presentation, the teacher observes each group. Each student in the presenting group must: 1) point to and state the location of at least two items using prepositions (e.g., "The pencil case is in the bag."); and 2) respond to a peer's question asking for clarification of a keyword (e.g., "What is a 'ruler'?" -> "It's for drawing straight lines." or "It's for measuring things."). The teacher will listen for use of /b/ and /t/ sounds and correct blending of prepositions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of vocabulary and prepositions (e.g., /b/ in "book", /t/ in "table", blending of "on", "in").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (classroom items, verbs, prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining keywords (simplifies terms, uses gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and facilitation of peer interactions (helps peers ask/answer questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses WH-questions and present simple correctly when describing.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I helped my friend understand ______ by ______ (e.g., 'pencil' by 'saying we write with it')."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain where ______ is. It is ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word or phrase you explained to a friend today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did you help your friends understand where the objects were in the classroom?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities effectively promoted mediation skills for 3rd graders? Did students demonstrate clear explanations using gestures and simple language? Were the peer interactions respectful and helpful? What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks, especially regarding vocabulary or sentence structure complexity at A1.1? Perhaps more visual aids or sentence frames could be used for specific explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes our "Discovering Our Classroom" theme. In the next unit, we will explore a new theme, applying our listening, reading, speaking, writing, and mediation skills to new vocabulary and contexts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>